cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this formalization, please cite it as below."
title: "Sunflower-Formal: a machine-checked Coq account of the Erdős–Rado sunflower problem"
type: software
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-01
repository-code: "https://github.com/thomas-falk-research/sunflower-formal"
authors:
  - family-names: "Falk"
    given-names: "Thomas"
    name-suffix: "III"
    alias: "thomas-falk-research"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9478-6061"
    email: "research@thomas-falk-research.com"
keywords:
  - Coq
  - formal verification
  - sunflower lemma
  - Erdős–Rado
  - Hall's marriage theorem
  - Kőnig's theorem
  - combinatorics
  - spread families
  - Alweiss-Lovett-Wu-Zhang
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A stdlib-only Coq 8.18 development around the Erdős–Rado sunflower
  problem, with a Rust search companion and a testing layer aimed at
  errors a proof kernel cannot catch. It contains the classical bounds
  f(n,k) <= (k-1)^n n! + 1 and f(n,k) >= (k-1)^n + 1, the exact value
  f(2,3) = 7, and constructive Hall and Kőnig theorems; the deterministic
  half of the 2020 Alweiss-Lovett-Wu-Zhang / Rao spread argument, with an
  axiom-free bound f(n,k) <= (n(k-1)+1)^n + 1; the Abbott-Hanson-Sauer
  (1972) substitution and iterated lower-bound rates for 3-sunflower-free
  and intersecting families; the conjecture for families of bounded
  VC-dimension; generic checkers for integer-programming bounds and for
  LRAT unsatisfiability proofs; and a kernel-checked bracket
  54 <= g(4) <= 76 whose upper end is conditional on a link census
  (validated by two independent exhaustive searches, not yet certified in
  the kernel) and a published meeting bound. The open Sunflower
  Conjecture is stated formally; this work does not resolve it and makes
  no progress on it in general. The only axiom in the library is the
  published 2020 spread lemma, quarantined in one file and used by one
  theorem. The proofs were developed with substantial AI assistance
  (Anthropic's Claude), directed and reviewed by the maintainer;
  correctness rests on the Coq kernel, not on how they were written.
